This field-ready checklist distills the draft playbook’s core ideas into seven practical actions for a digital product and community business. Its purpose is to turn audience attention into a transparent, useful customer journey—not pressure, confusion, or artificial urgency.

#align(center)[
  #text(size: 9pt, fill: luma(95))[Mark each item only after it is live, clear, and easy for a customer to understand.]
]

= The seven quick wins

== 1. Define one concrete outcome

□ Write a single sentence that says who the offer helps, what result it supports, and what the buyer receives.  
□ Make the deliverable visible before checkout: for example, an ebook, checklist, template pack, or scheduled live session.  
□ Remove vague outcome claims and avoid promising income, legal results, or guaranteed transformations.

> *Quick win:* A clear, productized outcome gives every post, sales page, and call to action one consistent message.

== 2. Build an honest limited-access moment

□ Use a real deadline, capped group size, or live-session cut-off only when it actually exists.  
□ State the reason for the limit and what happens after the window closes.  
□ Keep a standard option available wherever practical, so urgency never becomes concealment.

> *Source strategy:* The draft’s “Ticket Show Funnel” centers on time-sensitive access. Translate it into transparent event or cohort access rather than false scarcity.

== 3. Turn passive attention into a participation menu

□ Give viewers several clear ways to participate, from free poll/comment prompts to paid workshops or bonuses.  
□ Label each option with its value, price, and delivery method before a customer commits.  
□ Keep participation optional; customers should never be surprised by a charge or pressured to compete.

> *Source strategy:* The draft’s “Tip Menu Gamification” is strongest when it creates meaningful, clearly priced choices.

== 4. Recognize your highest-value contributors responsibly

□ Create non-monetary recognition such as member spotlights, early Q&A access, or community badges.  
□ If you run any paid competitive feature, disclose the rules, maximum cost, and benefit in advance.  
□ Reward contribution, insight, and helpfulness—not simply the largest spend.

> *Source strategy:* Reframe “King of the Hill” from a bidding contest into healthy, transparent community recognition.

#pagebreak()

= Build the system behind the offer

== 5. Create a lead magnet that earns the next step

□ Offer a free, useful entry asset tied directly to the paid product, such as this checklist, a starter framework, or a one-page planner.  
□ Deliver the lead magnet immediately and explain the next logical step without bait-and-switch tactics.  
□ Use the asset to learn which audience problem deserves the next product, LIVE, or Series lesson.

> *Source strategy:* The playbook identifies high-converting lead magnets as the bridge between traffic and a fuller offer.

== 6. Automate the essential customer path

□ Map the path: short-form video → bio link → product page → checkout → delivery → follow-up invitation.  
□ Test the path on mobile and remove any step that causes confusion or delays delivery.  
□ Automate only repetitive steps; retain human oversight for questions, refunds, and community safety.

> *Source strategy:* Empire building starts when a single offer is supported by dependable infrastructure rather than constant manual effort.

== 7. Run a measurable traffic rhythm

□ Publish with a repeating cadence that matches your capacity and gives every offer regular visibility.  
□ Use one primary call to action per post: either the low-ticket product or a community/Series invitation.  
□ Review views, profile visits, link clicks, conversion rate, refund rate, and audience feedback every week.

> *Source strategy:* The playbook frames traffic-generation pipelines as a core system—not an occasional promotion.

= Five-minute launch check

| Area | Before you publish, confirm |
|---|---|
| Offer | The outcome, deliverable, price, and delivery timing are unmistakable. |
| Access | Any deadline, limit, or scarcity message is factual and explained. |
| Checkout | The product page, checkout, and download work on a mobile phone. |
| Community | Participation rules are clear, respectful, and optional. |
| Traffic | Every post has one appropriate next step and a metric to review. |

